  "_provenance": {
    "captured_on": "2026-08-01",
    "what": "Two independent EDGAR expressions of the same acceptance instant for eight real filings. 'sgml_acceptance_datetime' is the verbatim <ACCEPTANCE-DATETIME> value from each filing's own -index-headers.html; 'submissions_acceptance_datetime' is the verbatim acceptanceDateTime string from data.sec.gov's submissions JSON for the same accession. No value here was computed, rounded or reformatted.",
    "observation": "For six of the eight, submissions_acceptance_datetime equals the SGML value read as America/New_York and converted to UTC (+5h under EST, +4h under EDT) - which is what proves the SGML value is Eastern local time. For the two filings under CIK 1750284 the submissions value instead repeats the Eastern wall clock with a 'Z' suffix. 17 CFR 232.13(a)(2) settles which reading is correct: 0001243233-24-000002 carries acceptance 18:55:18 and EDGAR filing date 2024-12-12, one business day after the acceptance date, which only follows if 18:55:18 is Eastern (after the 5:30 p.m. ET cut-off). Read as UTC it would be 13:55 ET and the filing date would have been 2024-12-11. The connector therefore derives knowledge_time from the SGML header and never from data.sec.gov."
  },
